@@ -0,0 +1,151 @@
+# ZapEE library integration
+
+**Zaptronics Embedded Electronics Library**
+
+## Decision
+
+Reusable Zaptronics chip drivers and ZapLink module implementations are moving
+out of the Hub repository into a separate GitHub repository:
+
+```text
+Zaptronics/zap-ee
+```
+
+The library has two first-class integration paths:
+
+1. standard CMake for Pico SDK, vendor CMake projects, bare-metal projects and
+   other CMake-based firmware;
+2. native Zephyr external-module integration through `zephyr/module.yml` and
+   Kconfig.
+
+The Hub remains a reference consumer of those libraries, not their owner.
+
+## Standard CMake contract
+
+A released Hub will pin an exact ZapEE tag with CMake FetchContent:
+
+```cmake
+include(FetchContent)
+
+FetchContent_Declare(
+    zapee
+    GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/Zaptronics/zap-ee.git
+    GIT_TAG        v0.1.0
+    GIT_SHALLOW    TRUE
+)
+FetchContent_MakeAvailable(zapee)
+
+target_link_libraries(Hub PRIVATE
+    zap::ztm_pwm
+)
+```
+
+The application names the board/module target it requires. `zap::ztm_pwm` owns
+its transitive chip-driver dependencies, so the Hub does not separately list
+SX1509, PAC195x, EMC2303, or any other implementation chip used by that board.
+
+If more than one module needs the same driver, CMake sees one target and builds
+one copy of that driver.
+
+## Local development
+
+During active development the Hub can use a local checkout without changing its
+`CMakeLists.txt` by overriding FetchContent at configure time:
+
+```text
+-DFETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_ZAPEE=V:/path/to/zap-ee
+```
+
+This is the intended development loop:
+
+```text
+Hub checkout --------------------+
+                                 |
+                                 +--> local zap-ee checkout
+                                 |      edit/test driver or module
+                                 |
+                                 +--> Hub integration test
+                                        |
+                                        +--> commit/tag library
+                                        +--> update pinned Hub tag
+```
+
+A later Hub build-script change may expose this CMake variable as a friendlier
+PowerShell argument once the first external module is actually consumed.
+
+## Zephyr contract
+
+The library repository contains:
+
+```text
+zephyr/module.yml
+zephyr/CMakeLists.txt
+zephyr/Kconfig
+```
+
+A Zephyr customer can add the repository to a west manifest (or supply it in
+`ZEPHYR_MODULES`) and select Zaptronics components through Kconfig. User-facing
+module options select their driver dependencies; the application should not
+need to enumerate fitted chips.
+
+## Target names
+
+Public CMake targets use the `zap::` namespace:
+
+```text
+zap::core
+zap::sx1509
+zap::pac195x
+zap::emc2303
+zap::ztm_pwm
+zap::ztm_adc
+...
+```
+
+One reusable component equals one CMake target.
+
+## Migration rule
+
+The current Hub repository still owns all existing `drivers/` and `modules/`
+source. They remain authoritative until each component is deliberately
+extracted and validated.
+
+For each migration:
+
+1. move the generic driver/module implementation into `zap-ee`;
+2. remove Pico/Hub-native types from the portable public API;
+3. add host-side tests for pure protocol/conversion logic where practical;
+4. add the CMake target and, when useful to Zephyr users, the Kconfig option;
+5. make the Hub consume the local external target;
+6. test on the physical Hub/module hardware;
+7. delete the duplicate Hub-owned source only after the external version works;
+8. tag the library and pin that tag in the Hub.
+
+At no stage should the same production driver be maintained independently in
+both repositories.
+
+## Platform/HAL boundary
+
+The library foundation intentionally does not freeze a transport ABI yet. The
+first real extractions will establish that contract from actual requirements.
+The agreed constraints are:
+
+- no Pico SDK, Zephyr, STM32 HAL, ESP-IDF, or other SDK-native types in portable
+  public driver/module headers;
+- explicit caller-owned instances and no hidden global state;
+- optional locking callbacks/operations so bare-metal users do not require an
+  RTOS;
+- bounded I2C/SPI operations with clear timeout semantics;
+- platform adapters for SDK-specific GPIO, buses, timing and locking;
+- host fakes must be possible for unit tests.
+
+Freezing these semantics after exercising the first several real drivers is
+preferable to inventing a generic HAL now and discovering later that serial
+chains, chip-select routing or module enable sequencing do not fit it.
+
+## Release policy
+
+ZapEE uses Semantic Versioning. Hub releases pin a release tag,
+not a moving branch. `0.x` is the API-extraction/stabilisation phase; `1.0.0`
+should wait until the platform contract and initial customer-facing module APIs
+are supportable as stable interfaces.
